  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\nPatchy low clouds have developed along the coast again this\nmorning with just a few high clouds elsewhere. Low clouds and\npatchy fog are forecast to spread northward along the coast and a\nfew miles inland through sunrise, clearing by mid-morning. Today\nwill be the last day of the heat wave with temperatures a few\ndegrees lower than yesterday. Highs will generally be in the low\nto mid 80s at the coast to the low 90s to low 100s for the inland\ncoastal areas and valleys, mid 80s to mid 90s in the lower\nelevation mountains, mid 70s to mid 80s for the higher elevation\nmountains, mid to upper 90s in the high desert, and 105 to 112 in\nthe low deserts. Monsoon moisture decreases today as flow aloft\nturns more southwesterly and precipitation chances this\nafternoon are less than 5%.\n\nFor Sunday through Wednesday, the upper level high which brought\nthe relentless heat retreats to the Southeast US as upper level\ntroughing develops along the West Coast. This pattern change\nwill bring gradual cooling to So Cal. By Sunday, highs will\nfinally return to late-August normals, falling to as much as 5 to\n10 degrees below normal by Wednesday. This drier flow pattern will\nalso offer some reprieve from the humidity and better cooling at\nnight. Say goodbye to dew points in the 70s (for now). For Tuesday\nand Wednesday, there is still the potential from mid and upper\nlevel moisture from Tropical Storm Karina (which is forecast to \nbecome a hurricane later today), to be pulled northward into \nCalifornia. The storm itself is forecast to remain well to our\nsouth. Global ensembles still disagree greatly with respect to \nthe amount of moisture that moves into our area. Some members of \nthe GEFS and Canadian have Precipitable Water in excess of 2\" and \nensemble means near 1.5\", while the EC ensemble is significantly \ndrier due to differences in the track of the tropical system. Even\nwith the increase in moisture, forcing will be weak, so if we do \nmanage to get any precipitation it will most likely be light. For \nnow PoPs remain around 15-25% for late Tuesday and Wednesday. \nOtherwise during this period expect the marine layer to rebuild \nand deepen, allowing low clouds and fog to spread slightly further\ninto the coastal areas and far western valleys during the nights \nand mornings.\n\nThursday into next weekend, the upper level high begins to shift\nback westward, bringing warming and the potential return of\nmonsoon moisture. High temperatures return to near average by\nFriday.",
